# The national unit for russia. Items take n=3; the world items reviewed here change by level.
# Dates are in engine/notation/chronicle.ts with their sources.
lesson history-g3-russia-our-country-long-ago v=1 format=teach grade=3 unit=9 subject=history nation=russia levels=[easy, medium, hard] {
  title "Old towns of the Golden Ring"
  goal "Put the first mentions of Russia's old towns in order, and say what a first mention in a chronicle means."

  look {
    scene 36x9 {
      timeline line from=800 to=1200 step=50 years=[862, 1024, 1147] labels=["Rostov first mentioned", "Suzdal first mentioned", "Moscow first mentioned"] at=canvas(0, 0)
    }
    say """
      Old monks wrote chronicles, books of what happened year by year. The chronicles name Rostov under the year 862, Yaroslavl about 1010, Suzdal in 1024 and Moscow in 1147. People lived in these places before, but a first mention is the oldest writing we have that names a town. Rostov, Suzdal, Yaroslavl and Vladimir are old towns of the Golden Ring, north-east of Moscow.
      """
  }
  do {
    level easy {
      worked history.n3-what n=3
    }
    show history.n3-order n=3 k=1 easy-k=0 hard-k=2
    show history.n3-between n=3 k=0 hard-k=1
    show history.n3-century n=3 k=0 hard-k=1
    show history.bc-ad-order k=2 easy-k=0 hard-k=4
    show history.across-year-one k=2 easy-k=0 hard-k=4
    show history.which-century k=2 easy-k=0 hard-k=4
    show history.n3-order n=3 k=2 easy-k=1 hard-k=0
    level medium hard {
      show history.n3-what n=3
    }
    level easy {
      show history.n3-century n=3 k=1
    }
    level hard {
      show history.bc-ad-order k=5
    }
  }
  story {
    show history.n3-between n=3 k=1 hard-k=0
  }
  try stars=2 {
    show history.n3-why n=3
  }
  try stars=3 {
    show history.n3-find-out n=3
  }
  remember {
    say "Every country's story goes back further than its writing. Where there is no writing, the ground and the buildings tell it."
  }

  grown-ups """
    The idea is to put the grade's methods (timelines across year one, centuries, evidence without
    writing) to work on the family's own country. The way in names what the place or thing was;
    the core orders three events of the country, counts the years between two, names a century,
    and reviews three of the grade's timeline questions; the two-star question asks how we know.
    The wrong idea to watch for is that a date in a book is when something began, when it is often
    only the first time it was written down or the oldest thing found. If the child is stuck, put
    the three events on a line you draw together. The three-star task is for you to mark: a small
    piece of finding out with you, near home. Russia's «Окружающий мир» in grade three and four takes children through the towns of their country and its past (recalled from the federal programme, not quoted here), and the chronicle's first mentions are the dates those towns keep.
    """
  level easy {
    grown-ups "At this level a worked example says what the place was, the events come nearly in order, the years counted are the closest pair, and the review questions stay on one side of year one."
  }
  level hard {
    grown-ups "At this level the events are fully muddled, the years counted are the longer pair, the century asked has a trap in it, and the review questions cross year one."
  }
}
